  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c3cac6cf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "No observations of juvenile snowshoe hares were made during 2002, while the highest count was registered in 1999 (the first year of the study) with 126 observations. In the previous plot we can see that the number of juvenile hare trappings dropped sharply from 1999 to 2002 and stayed lower for the rest of the study, with peaks in 2005, 2008, and 2011. \n",
    "\n",
    "<!-- In 2005 juvenile hare counts were 6 times higher than in 2004, and in 2008 counts were 11.4 times higher than in 2007. In 2011 the juvenile snowshoe hare count was 6.7 times higher than in 2010. -->\n",
    "\n",
    ":::{.callout-tip}\n",
    "# Exercise\n",
    "Consider the following question: In what ways could the data collection methods affect the results we see here?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "<!-- While these are absolute counts, these numbers are also impacted by effort. Moving forward I would suggest conducting the trappings on the same dates every year and space these times across different seasons. For example the first two weeks of each month every year. I imagine juveniles are mostly present during Spring, but with climate change it is possible Spring weather does not come at the same dates in 2012 as in 1999, so there might be a risk of undercounting if the counts are done during a fixed period of time every year. I would also suggest keeping the number and location of traps constant over the years. -->\n",
    "\n",
    "## Linear regression\n",
    "\n",
    "For our analysis we want to investigate possible relations between hind foot length and weight for juvenile hares.\n",
    "Let's take a look at our target data:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "024836c3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Number of juvenile hare captures with hind foot and weight measurements: 265\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<Axes: xlabel='hindft', ylabel='weight'>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 9,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 672x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Subset juvenile hares with hind foot and weight measurements\n",
    "juveniles_weight_hindft = hares_juv_adult[(hares_juv_adult['hindft'].notna()) & \n",
    "                                          (hares_juv_adult['weight'].notna()) &\n",
    "                                          (hares_juv_adult['age']=='juvenile')]\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Number of juvenile hare captures with hind foot and weight measurements: {len(juveniles_weight_hindft)}\")\n",
    "juveniles_weight_hindft.plot.scatter(x='hindft', y='weight', alpha=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9dc38cf1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can see there seems to be a linear trend between the weight and hind foot length variables, though as the hind foot length increases the variability does too. \n",
    "\n",
    "Let's investigate whether a linear model is an adequate way to describe this data. To do this we will use the [`LinearRegression`](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.linear_model.LinearRegression.html) model from the [`scikit-learn`](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/index.html) library. If you need a refresher about how a linear regression works, [click here](https://openintrostat.github.io/ims/model-slr.html) for a great resource. \n",
    "\n",
    "The trickiest part of fitting the model is to get the data in the required shape. From the [`LinearRegression` documentation](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.linear_model.LinearRegression.html) we see that:\n",
    "\n",
    "- input data (the x-values, independent variable, or training data) shape should be `(n_samples, 1)`. The 1 comes from having a single feature modeling the output data.\n",
    "- output data (the y-values, dependent variable, or target data) shape should be `(n_samples,)`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's select and reshape the input and output data accordingly:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "952a1e9d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Input data shape: (265, 1)\n",
      "Output data shape: (265,)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# x-values\n",
    "X = np.array(juveniles_weight_hindft['hindft']).reshape((-1, 1))   # Reshape to (n_samples, 1)\n",
    "print(f\"Input data shape: {X.shape}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# y-values\n",
    "y = np.array(juveniles_weight_hindft['weight'])    # Shape is (n_samples,)\n",
    "print(f\"Output data shape: {y.shape}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7b76db5a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we create a `LinearRegression` instance and fit it to our data to create a `model`. From the `model` we extract the coefficient of determination, or $R^2$, and the $y$-intercept and slope from our linear model. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "id": "a2c15313",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Coefficient of determination (R^2): 0.3871164783665735\n",
      "y-intercept: -352.77198564640685\n",
      "Slope: 10.092239638623013\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Fit model\n",
    "model = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Calculate R^2\n",
    "R_sq = model.score(X, y)\n",
    "print(f\"Coefficient of determination (R^2): {R_sq}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Retrieve intercept and slope\n",
    "intercept = model.intercept_\n",
    "print(f\"y-intercept: {intercept}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "slope = model.coef_[0]\n",
    "print(f\"Slope: {slope}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "95724326",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Remember that the equation of the linear model is given by\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\hat{y} = \\hat\\beta_0 + \\hat\\beta_1 x, $$\n",
    "\n",
    "where\n",
    "\n",
    "- $x$ = input variable\n",
    "- $\\hat{y}$ = **estimated $y$ value** at $x$ from the linear model \n",
    "- $\\hat\\beta_0$ = the **$y$-intercept** of the linear model, this is interpreted as the estimated average value of $y$ when $x=0$\n",
    "- $\\hat\\beta_1$ = the **slope** of the linear model, this is the estimated difference in the predicted value $\\hat{y}$ per unit of $x$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **coefficient of determination**, $R^2$, is between 0 and 1, with $R^2=1$ describing a perfect fit of the model to the data. The $R^2$ value is interpreted as the proportion of variance in the outcome variable $y$ that is explained by the least squares line with the variable $x$. \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "We can use all this information to plot our linear model together with our data:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "d777c607",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 768x576 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| code-fold: True\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8,6))\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.scatter(x=juveniles_weight_hindft['hindft'], \n",
    "           y=juveniles_weight_hindft['weight'], \n",
    "           alpha=0.5,\n",
    "           color='#8FBC8F')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define parameters of the regression line\n",
    "x_start = juveniles_weight_hindft['hindft'].min()\n",
    "x_end = juveniles_weight_hindft['hindft'].max()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the line\n",
    "ax.plot([x_start, x_end], \n",
    "        [slope*x_start + intercept, slope*x_end + intercept],\n",
    "        color=\"darkred\", \n",
    "        lw=2, \n",
    "        linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add the parameters\n",
    "beta0 = r'$intercept = \\hat\\beta_0 =$'+ str(round(intercept,2))\n",
    "ax.text(80, 1300, beta0, fontsize=10, horizontalalignment='center')\n",
    "\n",
    "beta1 = r'$slope = \\hat\\beta_1 =$' + str(round(slope,2))\n",
    "ax.text(80, 1200, beta1, fontsize=10, horizontalalignment='center')\n",
    "\n",
    "r_squared = r'$R^2 =$' + str(round(R_sq,2))\n",
    "ax.text(80, 1100, r_squared, fontsize=10, horizontalalignment='center')        \n",
    "\n",
    "# Add a title and axis label\n",
    "ax.set_title('Relationship between hind foot length and weight of \\n juvenile snowshoe hares at Bonanza Creek Experimental Forest',\n",
    "             {'fontsize':15})\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Hind foot length (mm)')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Weight (g)')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show the plot\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f33fe89e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Checking the residuals\n",
    "\n",
    "$R^2$ tells us how much of the variation the model explains, but not whether a straight line is the right shape for the data. To check this, we look at the **residuals**. A residual is the difference between an observed value and the value predicted by the model:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$e_i = y_i - \\hat{y}_i.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "If a linear model describes the data well, the residuals should be scattered randomly around 0, with about the same spread for every value of $x$. Let's calculate the residuals and plot them against the hind foot length:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "61365675",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 768x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Calculate residuals: observed minus predicted weight\n",
    "residuals = y - model.predict(X)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.scatter(x=juveniles_weight_hindft['hindft'],\n",
    "           y=residuals,\n",
    "           alpha=0.5,\n",
    "           color='#8FBC8F')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Reference line at 0\n",
    "ax.axhline(0, color=\"darkred\", lw=2, linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title('Residuals of the linear model', {'fontsize':15})\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Hind foot length (mm)')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Residual (g)')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a32be0ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The residuals show three patterns:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The spread grows with hind foot length.** For short hind feet most residuals are within about 100 g of 0, while for the longest hind feet many are more than 300 g away. This is the increasing variability we noticed in the first scatter plot. It means the model's predictions are less precise for larger juveniles.\n",
    "- **The residuals are not centered on 0 everywhere.** For hind feet shorter than about 100 mm most residuals are negative (the line overestimates weight), while for medium hind feet they are mostly positive (the line underestimates weight). This suggests that the relationship between hind foot length and weight is not exactly linear.\n",
    "- **For longer hind feet the residuals split into two groups**, one above and one below the line. This could mean that another variable also affects weight. Exercise (c) below asks you to think about which one.\n",
    "\n",
    "None of these patterns means the model is useless: the line still captures the overall trend. But they are a reminder that a single $R^2$ value doesn't tell the whole story, and that we should be careful when using the model to make predictions, especially for larger juveniles.\n",
    "\n",
